<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04F4">Corollary <span data-tag="04F4">8.6.3.14</span> <span class="named">(Uniqueness)</span>.</a> Let $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a cocartesian fibration of simplicial sets. Then $U$ admits a cartesian conjugate, which is uniquely determined up equivalence. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Combining Propositions <a data-tag="04F2" href="/tag/04F2">8.6.3.11</a> and <a data-tag="05JK" href="/tag/05JK">8.6.3.5</a>, we see that a cartesian fibration $U^{\dagger }: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}^{\dagger } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}}$ is conjugate to $U$ if and only if it is equivalent to the projection map $\operatorname{Cospan}^{\dagger }( \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}/\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}}$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
